# CRON(8)

## NAME

cron - clock daemon

## SYNOPSIS

```c
/etc/cron
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Cron* executes commands at specified dates and times according to the instructions in the file
/usr/lib/crontab. Since *cron* never exits, it should only be executed once. This is best done
by running *cron* from the initialization process through the file /etc/rc; see init(8).

Crontab consists of lines of six fields each. The fields are separated by spaces or tabs. The
first five are integer patterns to specify the minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of the month
(1-31), month of the year (1-12), and day of the week (0-6 with 0=Sunday). Each of these
patterns may contain a number in the range above; two numbers separated by a minus meaning a
range inclusive; a list of numbers separated by commas meaning any of the numbers; or an
asterisk meaning all legal values. The sixth field is a string that is executed by the Shell at
the specified times. A percent character in this field is translated to a new-line character,
and the whole string, newlines and all, is given to the Shell — so every line of it is executed.

Note: v7's page said the day of the week is "1-7 with 1=monday" and that only the first line of
the command field is executed by the Shell, the rest being made available to the command as
standard input. Both were wrong about v7's own program, and neither has been made true here. The
day of the week is compared against the field ctime(3) fills in, which counts 0 through 6 from
Sunday; and there is no pipe anywhere in *cron* — the standard input of a job is /dev/null.

A line that cannot be parsed is ignored without a diagnostic, which is also how a comment
works: a line whose first non-blank character is `#` is skipped. A last line with no terminating
newline is dropped.

Crontab is examined by *cron* every minute, and re-read whenever its modification time has
changed.

Note: commands run as the super-user. v7's *cron* did setuid(1) before forking anything, which
on this system cannot work: the one command /usr/lib/crontab actually runs is /usr/lib/atrun,
and atrun needs the privilege of setuid(2) to give each at(1) job the identity of whoever
submitted it. Since /usr/lib/crontab is mode 0644 and owned by the super-user, nobody but the
super-user can put a command there in the first place. See at(1).

Note: a job's standard output and standard error are /dev/null. Anything to be seen must be
redirected by the command itself — to /dev/console, for instance.

## FILES

/usr/lib/crontab, mode 0644 and owned by the super-user

## SEE ALSO

at(1), init(8), sh(1)
